WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.020
Greta Gerwig is bringing Barbie to life on
the big screen, but is a movie about a doll

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.510
just toying around, or will it somehow break
the mold?

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:13.059
Here's all you need to know before checking
out the latest toy-turned-blockbuster.

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:17.900
If you're a movie lover, then you've no doubt
circled July 21 on your calendar.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.570
After all, it's the most important day of
2023.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:21.570
Why's that?

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.880
"How about because this is the most important
thing to ever happen in the history of the

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:25.880
world!?!"

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.600
On that day, two phenomenal films will hit
theaters at the same time and go head-to-head

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.410
for box office dominance.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:37.250
July 21 will play host to "Barbie" — a colorful,
zany comedy — and Christopher Nolan's "Oppenheimer"

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.250
— a grim, heavy biopic.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.950
Regardless of which film does better, it's
a win-win for cinephiles, as both films look

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.500
like they're going to be absolutely exceptional.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.800
When it comes to the movie's plot, the official
"Barbie" website has provided a short-but-sweet

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:51.800
summary, which reads,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.050
"To live in Barbie Land is to be a perfect
being in a perfect place.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.450
Unless you have a full-on existential crisis.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.450
Or you're a Ken."

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:03.829
Unfortunately for our ever-smiling hero, it
looks like she's having that crisis.

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:07.780
In the film's official main trailer, we see
how her perfect life is falling apart.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.750
Her morning shower is ruined by a sudden surge
of cold water.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.850
While she used to easily float down from the
roof of her house into her cute Barbie car,

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.200
now she's just falling straight to the ground.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.900
And instead of feet that always perfectly
mimic the shape of a high heel shoe, suddenly,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.240
her soles are flat on the ground — the horror.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.350
"FLAT FEET!

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:28.400
BLECH!"

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.960
Most alarming of all, Barbie is suddenly haunted
by thoughts of death.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.350
Hoping to find answers to life's big questions,
she decides to venture out into the real world,

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.530
with a stowaway Ken tagging along.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.330
Unfortunately, this is frowned upon by the
executives at Mattel, the company that makes

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.380
Barbie toys.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.310
When our blonde hero leaves Barbie Land behind,
the suits do everything in their power to

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.540
hunt her down.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.700
Will they put her back in the box before Barbie
can figure her life out?

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.670
The trailers for "Barbie" have all been pretty
incredible.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:59.950
The first "Barbie" teaser was a hilarious
homage to "2001: A Space Odyssey," with a

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:04.340
tribe of little girls ditching their baby
dolls when a gigantic Barbie shows up on the

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.990
scene, standing in for "2001's" eerie monolith.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.060
The second "Barbie" teaser got even zanier,
complete with Beach Boys tunes, the bright

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.830
pink hues of Barbie Land, and every character
greeting our bubbly blonde protagonist.

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:15.830
"Hi, Barbie!"

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:16.830
"Hi Barbie!"

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:17.830
"Hi Barbie!"

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.270
However, it's the film's main trailer that
really drives home the plot — showing the

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.610
cracks that are starting to show in Barbie's
seemingly perfect life.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:28.780
With thoughts of death swirling around her
head, Barbie is given a "Matrix"-like choice

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:30.330
by Kate McKinnon's character.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:34.340
She can either pick the high heel and stay
in her regular life or pick the sandal and

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.590
go on a journey of exploration.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.811
After a bit of prodding, Barbie and Ken set
out on a highly stylized journey to the real

00:02:40.811 --> 00:02:41.811
world.

00:02:41.811 --> 00:02:44.830
Of course, as the trailer shows, the real
world is no cake walk.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:46.250
Barbie even winds up in jail!

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:50.360
However, as she searches for meaning, Barbie
never gives up hope, and the trailer promises

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.290
that this wacky comedy is going to go to some
serious places.

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.480
"Ideas live forever."

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.530
"Barbie" features one of the best casts we've
seen in a long time.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:05.129
Margot Robbie stars as the titular doll, which
is perfect casting, as she looks exactly like

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:06.129
Barbie.

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:09.980
And since Robbie has played wildly varied
characters in projects like "The Suicide Squad,"

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.140
"I, Tonya," and "Once Upon a Time in Hollywood,"
we know her spin on the iconic character will

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.140
be something unique.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:18.989
Of course, you can't have Barbie without Ken,
and Ryan Gosling is stepping into the role

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:23.959
of the blonde boy toy, which is another casting
triumph, since Gosling looks like a walking,

00:03:23.959 --> 00:03:24.959
talking Ken doll.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:29.599
And while he excels at playing quiet, brooding
men, he's also great at over-the-top comedy,

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:31.080
as evidenced by "The Nice Guys."

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.780
"I had to question the mermaids!

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.110
What were you doing while I was working?"

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:38.959
As for the rest of the cast, this is where
things get incredibly funny.

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:42.819
Most of the actresses are all playing different
versions of Barbie, and most of the actors

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:44.740
are playing various versions of Ken.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:49.799
For example, Issa Rae is President Barbie,
Ritu Arya is a Pulitzer-Prize–winning Barbie,

00:03:49.799 --> 00:03:52.130
and Dua Lipa is Mermaid Barbie.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.879
Playing a whole host of Kens — whose defining
characteristic seems to be the fact that they're

00:03:55.879 --> 00:04:01.360
all just Kens — we've got Simu Liu, Kingsley
Ben-Adir, Ncuti Gatwa, and Scott Evans, just

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:02.510
to name a few Kens.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:07.160
Michael Cera of "Arrested Development" fame
also lives in Barbie Land, but he's a bit

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.160
of an oddball.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.760
Instead of playing a Ken, Cera is playing
Allan, a character based on a discontinued

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.100
1960s toy that was made to be Ken's best friend.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:19.549
Similarly, Oscar-winning screenwriter Emerald
Fennell will play Midge, another less-famous

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:22.730
toy who was at one point canonically married
to Allan.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:26.890
Rounding things out, Will Ferrell will play
the CEO of Mattel, Helen Mirren will narrate

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:31.830
the movie, Rhea Perlman of "Cheers" fame will
also show up, and famed wrestler-turned-actor

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.210
John Cena will reportedly appear as a merman.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:40.050
"Barbie" marks Greta Gerwig's third film as
a director, but while she's only helmed three

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.539
movies, Gerwig is one of the most exciting
filmmakers in Tinseltown.

00:04:43.539 --> 00:04:47.550
She first sat down behind the camera for "Lady
Bird," the coming-of-age dramedy that earned

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:51.840
multiple Oscar nominations, including one
for best picture and two for Gerwig — best

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.949
director and best original screenplay.

00:04:53.949 --> 00:04:58.259
She then reunited with Saoirse Ronan for a
beloved adaptation of "Little Women," which

00:04:58.259 --> 00:05:02.620
earned a best picture nod at the Academy Awards
and also nabbed Gerwig an Oscar nomination

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.020
for best adapted screenplay.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.050
In other words, when Gerwig gets involved
with a project, you can bet that film will

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.610
turn out to be gold — even a concept as
crazy as "Barbie."

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:16.220
While a guest on Dua Lipa's podcast, "At Your
Service," Gerwig talked about her experience

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:17.900
making the upcoming film, saying,

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.150
"It was terrifying.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:23.050
[…] That feeling that I had was knowing
that it would be really interesting terror.

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:24.950
Usually that's where the best stuff is.

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.280
[...] Anything where you're like, 'This could
be a career-ender,' then you're like, 'Okay,

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.740
I probably should do it.'"

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:33.850
Gerwig also wrote the script, alongside Noah
Baumbach, her romantic partner.

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:38.090
Similar to Gerwig, Baumbach is also a highly
respected director, having helmed movies like

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.889
"Marriage Story" and "The Squid and the Whale."

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:45.310
The Barbie Dreamhouse is a legendary estate,
and the "Barbie" movie design team has done

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:48.460
the palace extra proud — and extra pink.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:52.290
Production design and set decoration dream
team Sarah Greenwood and Katie Spencer drew

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:57.040
inspiration from mid-century modern mansions
and real-life toys, plus director Gerwig's

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.660
references — which, according to Architectural
Digest, range from the bold "Pee-wee's Big

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:05.199
Adventure" to the pastel-powered fever dream
of "An American in Paris."

00:06:05.199 --> 00:06:09.020
The result is a Dreamhouse that looks like
"Mad Men" meets Nicki Minaj.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.870
The design of the Dreamhouse is inspired by
"the Kaufmann House," a Palm Springs home

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.870
designed in 1946 by Richard Neutra.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:20.000
While Neutra's work favors neutral shades,
Greenwood and Spencer had a mandate of their

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.120
own to follow: think pink.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:26.690
The team used so much pink paint it caused
an international shortage, but not all of

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.830
that paint was on the Dreamhouse itself.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:33.200
The beautiful backdrops that surround Barbie
Land are hand-painted, not CGI, partly in

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:37.419
keeping with what Gerwig calls the "authentic
artificiality" of Barbie's world.

00:06:37.419 --> 00:06:40.630
The home is full of bright colors and toy-like
details.

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:44.990
Even Barbie's closet is clearly designed to
match the packaging of clothes sold separately.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:49.389
Still, a quick glance at Barbie's movie home
shows that there is a price to be paid for

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:51.000
living a "perfect" life.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.120
Gerwig explained to Architectural Digest,

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:56.610
"​​Dreamhouses assume that you never have
anything you wish was private.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.039
There is no place to hide."

00:06:59.039 --> 00:07:03.919
Barbie and Batman have a lot in common: names
that start with "B," extreme wealth and style,

00:07:03.919 --> 00:07:08.449
absentee parents — so it only makes sense
that they also share the same taste in automobiles.

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:12.479
The "Barbie" movie's Dream Car was designed
by the same artists who made the Batmobile

00:07:12.479 --> 00:07:14.419
for Robert Pattinson's "The Batman."

00:07:14.419 --> 00:07:19.470
While this batty collaboration has us hoping
for an influx of street-legal hot pink Batmobiles,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:24.680
we won't hold our breath — unless it's for
another potential, electric, and real-life

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:25.680
Barbie car tie-in.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.990
Barbie's movie ride is a custom pink electric
Corvette.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:33.870
Eagle-eyed viewers of the "Barbie" trailers
can spot an electric charging port and a blue-highlighted

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.270
"EV" in the "Chevrolet" script on the side
of the Dream Car.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:41.069
When Fox News reached out to Chevrolet regarding
possible real-life Barbie cars, Chevrolet

00:07:41.069 --> 00:07:42.340
provided a response:

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:46.870
"Barbie and Corvette have had a long-standing
relationship and we're excited to build on

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:48.120
that in the 'Barbie' movie.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.550
More to come on this and some other surprises."

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:55.229
Autoevolution reports that GM has confirmed
that electric Corvettes are slated for release

00:07:55.229 --> 00:07:58.900
soon — so why shouldn't some be pretty in
pink?

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:02.169
Barbie and pink go together like, well, Barbie
and Ken.

00:08:02.169 --> 00:08:07.110
But production design and set decorator collaborators
Sarah Greenwood and Katie Spencer had to get

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:11.470
the right pinks for the "Barbie" movie — pinks
that would sustain the dreaminess of Barbie's

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:15.759
world, as well as convey the playful, toy-like
quality of her "real" life.

00:08:15.759 --> 00:08:18.210
Finding the perfect pink was no easy task.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:22.420
The designers narrowed their scope from 100
pinks to a range of 10 to build the look of

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:23.430
the film.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:27.949
According to IndieWire, Gerwig named the color
palette "Techni Barbie," in reference to "Technicolor,"

00:08:27.949 --> 00:08:32.680
a suite of film color processes that was popular
with movie musicals like "The Wizard of Oz"

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:33.779
and "The Red Shoes."

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:39.290
Rodrigo Prieto, who shot "Brokeback Mountain"
and "The Irishman," is no stranger to colorful

00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:40.290
films.

00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:42.529
He also shot "The Wolf of Wall Street" and
"Broken Embraces."

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:46.160
His artistry across styles is put to use by
the "Barbie" movie.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:49.700
While we barely get a glimpse of the "real"
world in the "Barbie" movie, one thing's for

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:54.220
sure — when Barbie's there, she's not in
pink Kansas anymore.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:58.950
The only way Barbie can have a "giant party
with planned choreography and a bespoke song"

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:01.709
every night in Barbie Land is with a stacked
soundtrack.

00:09:01.709 --> 00:09:08.060
So far, Nicki Minaj, Ice Spice, Lizzo, Kid
LAROI, HAIM, Charlie XCX, Tame Impala, Ava

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:13.209
Max, Dominic Fike, KAROL G, PinkPantheress,
and FIFTY FIFTY are confirmed to grace the

00:09:13.209 --> 00:09:16.019
soundtrack with their pink and powerful presences.

00:09:16.019 --> 00:09:20.370
Maybe we'll even get more of Aqua than the
sample used in the movie trailer implies.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:25.100
The album is executive produced by hitmaker
Mark Ronson, who personally requested Dua

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:28.079
Lipa work on a song for a major set piece
in the movie.

00:09:28.079 --> 00:09:32.560
According to his own Instagram post, Mark
Ronson DM'd Dua Lipa a request to work on

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.770
the movie, calling the movie "hilarious."

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.740
Dua's song will score a 60-person dance scene
in the movie.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.089
"Dance the Night" also has its own music video,
featuring a cameo by Greta Gerwig.

00:09:42.089 --> 00:09:46.610
While the "Barbie News"-themed "Barbie the
Album" cover promises "more Barbies and Kens

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:51.240
to be announced," there's one more Ken musician
we're excited about — the Kennest Ken of

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.220
them all, Ryan Gosling.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:56.839
While this might shock some fans of the actor,
others might be familiar with his musical

00:09:56.839 --> 00:10:02.149
side from "La La Land" and "Lars and the Real
Girl," or his former band Dead Man's Bones.

00:10:02.149 --> 00:10:06.000
Real ones know Gosling's come a long way from
his "Mickey Mouse Club" days, even if his

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.600
Ken costumes beg to differ.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:12.320
Every interview Ryan Gosling grants about
playing Ken in the "Barbie" movie is better

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:13.550
than the last.

00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:18.029
Even though, according to the movie, Ken's
job is "just beach," Gosling had a hard time

00:10:18.029 --> 00:10:19.740
getting a handle on his character at first.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.110
He told the crowd at CinemaCon,

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:25.660
"I didn't know Ken from within and if I'm
being really honest, I doubted my Kenergy.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:29.810
I didn't see it, but Margot and Greta, they
conjured this out of me somehow.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:33.670
It was like I was living my life and then
one day I was bleaching my hair, shaving my

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:37.170
legs, wearing bespoke neon outfits, and rollerblading
down Venice beach."

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:41.770
Gosling also spoke at CinemaCon about how
Robbie helped him tap into his elusive Kenergy,

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:42.770
saying,

00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:46.820
"She left a pink present with a pink bow,
from Barbie to Ken, every day while we were

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:47.820
filming.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:48.820
They were all beach-related.

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:51.540
Like puka shells, or a sign that says 'Pray
for surf.'"

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:56.070
If Gerwig and Robbie helped Gosling achieve
internal mental Ken fitness, he was on his

00:10:56.070 --> 00:11:00.190
own to attain Ken's considerable physical
fitness, which was no mean feat.

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:05.590
"That Ken life is even harder than 'The Gray
Man' life, I think."

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:09.710
Even before the "Barbie" movie's release,
the brand's iconic aesthetic inspired a fashion

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:11.260
trend called "Barbiecore."

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:16.730
Think pink — like, neon pink — and a playful,
poppy aesthetic that leans into frills, "girly"

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:19.410
energy, and all things toy-like and fabulous.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:24.430
A quick search of "Barbiecore" on TikTok returns
gazillions of views on posts featuring looks

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:25.740
popping with pink.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:29.990
Pastel, shimmer, magenta, fuschia, neon — the
pink sky's the limit.

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:34.430
Vogue reports that the look has taken over
runway fashion as well, especially with Versace's

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:37.290
recent sparkling (and movie-ready) fashions.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:41.221
Tourism boards are taking advantage of the
sudden interest in "Barbiecore" as well, with

00:11:41.221 --> 00:11:45.709
California creating a "Barbiecore Tour Through
California" campaign for those who prefer

00:11:45.709 --> 00:11:48.570
their pink with a touch of '60s-inflected
style.

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:52.279
Some "Barbiecore" is even a direct result
of the meta-world of the movie.

00:11:52.279 --> 00:11:55.980
When Barbie leaves behind Barbie Land for
the "real" world, she and Ken skate through

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:00.930
Venice Beach on skates they brought from home,
based on the iconic blades of '90s Barbies.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:05.269
Impala Skate is releasing tie-in skates and
pads that exactly match the ones Barbie and

00:12:05.269 --> 00:12:06.640
Ken wear in the movie.

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.200
Soon, real-life Barbies and Kens can buy their
own to live their own plastic and fantastic

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:14.730
Barbiecore lifestyle — if they aren't already
sold out.

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:19.990
Issa Rae, who plays President Barbie in the
"Barbie" movie, is a legendary writer, director,

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:21.350
actor, and producer.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:25.500
Known for making and starring in hit shows
like "Insecure," Rae knows a thing or two

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.709
about creating powerful pop culture — and
good leadership.

00:12:28.709 --> 00:12:32.699
Rae has openly praised the energy and leadership
on the "Barbie" set.

00:12:32.699 --> 00:12:33.740
Rae told PopSugar,

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:37.389
"Literally everybody on that set was just
great vibes, great to be around.

00:12:37.389 --> 00:12:38.750
It starts at the top...

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:42.210
You have two people, your lead and your director,
who are both women, I must say.

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:45.210
They're just both incredible people and incredibly
talented.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:46.740
They made it a comfort zone."

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:50.411
Initially, Rae wasn't fully on board with
Gerwig's pitch for the Barbie movie, just

00:12:50.411 --> 00:12:52.699
the director's enthusiasm for the idea.

00:12:52.699 --> 00:12:55.060
But when Rae got to the script, she told The
Hollywood Reporter,

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:58.480
​​"Reading it was, like, 'Oh my God, I
love her even more.'

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:03.209
And then actually shooting it, it was incredible,
one of my favorite experiences."

00:13:03.209 --> 00:13:08.149
Will Ferrell plays the CEO of Mattel in the
film, who so far looks like the movie's villain.

00:13:08.149 --> 00:13:12.930
While Ferrell isn't exactly a victim of CEO
typecasting, this isn't the first time Ferrell

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:17.079
has played a slightly villainous institutional
figure that breaks the laws of reality while

00:13:17.079 --> 00:13:18.639
wearing a suit and tie.

00:13:18.639 --> 00:13:22.930
Ferrell plays a Dad who just doesn't understand
his kid in "The LEGO Movie" — until, of

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:26.230
course, his kid and his LEGO remind him of
the power of play.

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:30.800
His costume for "Barbie" is incredibly similar
to that in "The LEGO Movie," except in "Barbie"

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.610
his tie is pink, of course.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:34.920
Perhaps the movie's themes share a common
thread?

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:39.720
Ferrell has also played the wildly creative
fashion designer overlord Mugatu, the silly

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:41.579
and villainous character in "Zoolander."

00:13:41.579 --> 00:13:46.339
Ferrell is good at playing over-the-top, yet
grounded characters, whether in suit-and-tie

00:13:46.339 --> 00:13:47.339
or haute couture.

00:13:47.339 --> 00:13:50.029
He adored being in "Barbie," telling the Wall
Street Journal,

00:13:50.029 --> 00:13:54.170
"It's a loving homage to the brand and, at
the same time, couldn't be more satirical

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:59.079
[...] Just an amazing comment on male patriarchy
and women in society and why Barbie's criticized

00:13:59.079 --> 00:14:01.670
and yet why every little girl still wants
to play with Barbie."

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:06.800
Barbie has been in development for a long
time, first with Amy Schumer set to star,

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:08.920
and later with Anne Hathaway attached.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:13.089
Some time after that, Margot Robbie and her
husband's production company, LuckyChap, scooped

00:14:13.089 --> 00:14:14.089
up the property.

00:14:14.089 --> 00:14:18.240
While it might seem obvious that Robbie would
consider herself an iconic blonde, for the

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:22.110
role of an iconic blonde, she actually wasn't
her own first choice.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:24.160
Robbie tells The Hollywood Reporter about
LuckyChap,

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:27.420
"We never started a company to be a starring
vehicle for me.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:31.899
We wanted to expand what female stories and
female storytellers could do in this industry,

00:14:31.899 --> 00:14:34.170
and I don't need to be onscreen for that to
happen."

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:38.610
So it tracks that Robbie initially thought
Gal Gadot would be a perfect fit for the role

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:41.779
of the beautiful, sweet, and super-sincere
doll.

00:14:41.779 --> 00:14:42.779
Robbie tells Vogue,

00:14:42.779 --> 00:14:44.430
"Gal Gadot is Barbie energy.

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:49.100
She's so genuinely sincere, and she's so enthusiastically
kind, that it's almost dorky.

00:14:49.100 --> 00:14:50.839
It's like right before being a dork."

00:14:50.839 --> 00:14:55.420
While the story of the "Barbie" movie has
been kept mostly mysterious leading up to

00:14:55.420 --> 00:15:00.550
its release, it's clearly meant to be a magical,
existential tale meant to appeal to kids and

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:01.550
adults.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:05.180
While Gerwig and the rest of the cast and
crew have playfully only teased that "Barbie"

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:09.920
is going to subvert expectations, Greta has
shared some of her more philosophical inspirations:

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:12.860
a trip to Mattel and an old book.

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:16.170
Gerwig tells Vogue about "meeting" the many
versions of Barbie at Mattel,

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:19.100
"All of [the dolls] are Barbie, and Barbie
is everyone.

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:21.889
Philosophically, I was like, Well, now that's
interesting."

00:15:21.889 --> 00:15:26.380
The amount and variety of Barbies inspired
Gerwig, who said it gave her "an expansive

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:28.819
idea of self that we could all learn from."

00:15:28.819 --> 00:15:33.910
Gerwig also shared with Vogue that a parenting
guide book she read as a kid, "Reviving Ophelia,"

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:36.029
kept coming up when she was writing "Barbie."

00:15:36.029 --> 00:15:39.970
The book is about raising teenage girls and
the depression they may face during the changes

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:41.350
of adolescence.

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:42.950
Gerwig related that to Barbie's, saying,

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:45.750
"How is this journey the same thing that a
teenage girl feels?

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:48.519
All of a sudden, she thinks, Oh, I'm not good
enough."

00:15:48.519 --> 00:15:50.890
"Do you guys ever think about dying?"

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:55.779
Margot Robbie and Greta Gerwig know that Barbie
as a toy is beloved and be-hated in equal

00:15:55.779 --> 00:15:56.779
measure.

00:15:56.779 --> 00:16:00.899
Barbie is a significant cultural figure, sometimes
for good and sometimes for bad.

00:16:00.899 --> 00:16:05.569
She is a symbol of empowerment, adventure,
and acceptance — as well as, at times, body

00:16:05.569 --> 00:16:09.210
image issues, impossible beauty standards,
and vapidity.

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:12.110
Robbie and Gerwig decided to use all of that
in their movie.

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:13.110
Robbie told Vogue,

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:16.569
"We have to acknowledge that there are a lot
of people who aren't fans of Barbie.

00:16:16.569 --> 00:16:18.199
They actively hate Barbie.

00:16:18.199 --> 00:16:19.870
And have a real issue with Barbie.

00:16:19.870 --> 00:16:21.949
We need to find a way to acknowledge that."

00:16:21.949 --> 00:16:26.170
While it remains to be seen exactly how the
not-so-sparkly side of Barbie will be handled

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:30.070
in the movie, the thoughtful and playful sound
bites sound promising.

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:34.089
The sense of play the entire creative team
has been showcasing has audiences feeling

00:16:34.089 --> 00:16:35.419
like kids again.

